WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.240
Surviving a cement truck, nearly missing their
big break, and banning jokes about … bacon?

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.309
Turns out there’s a whole lot you probably
didn’t know about the wild and wacky slackers

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:10.949
behind “Workaholics!”

00:00:11.064 --> 00:00:16.030
As the sketch comedians behind "Workaholics"
went from internet notoriety to TV breakouts,

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.650
they proved that they were anything but the
slackers they portrayed on TV, diversifying

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.810
themselves on the show by earning credits
as directors, writers and producers.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:27.170
Anders Holm took the boss's chair for a few
episodes throughout the series, starting with

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.330
the Season 4 classic "Best Buds."

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.520
DeVine later stepped up to direct an episode
that he had also written.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.180
But one tidbit that might surprise the more
casual fan is that the show's drug-dealing

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.640
homeless man, in actuality, was the show's
primary showrunner, directing more than half

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.640
of the series.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.000
Kyle Newacheck, known as Karl on "Workaholics,"
graduated from Los Angeles Film School and

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.890
directed most of the crew's online sketch
routines.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:52.430
Since the conclusion of "Workaholics," in
fact, his star has only soared higher.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:56.570
Newacheck has gone on in the years since to
direct episodes of popular comedy shows like

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.120
"Community" and "Parks and Recreation," and
was recently nominated for an Emmy for his

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.699
work on "What We Do in the Shadows."

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:05.930
Not content with just series work, Newacheck
also directed the Adam Sandler-led Netflix

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:10.130
hit "Murder Mystery," as well as "Game Over,
Man," which starred the "Workaholics" crew.

00:01:10.378 --> 00:01:14.240
The "Workaholics" crew were so determined
to avoid cliches at all costs that they kept

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.600
track of pitfalls whenever they arose.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.420
By the final season, they had crafted two
jam-packed, hilariously moan-worthy whiteboards

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:24.320
of joke cliches, determined to avoid them
at all costs.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.080
In 2016, executive producer John Quaintance
posted a photo of the whiteboards on Twitter

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.580
as a public service not only for other comedy
writers, but for millions of viewers who never,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.470
ever wanted to hear another joke involving
the phrase, quote, "awesome sauce."

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.640
"Hey I love you."

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.630
"Dude shut up!

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.430
That is awesome sauce!"

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:46.390
The boards included tons of cliched phrases
that have popped up in sitcoms, but perhaps

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:51.250
the highlight of the entire list, however,
was one small notation that simply read - "Bacon anything."

00:01:51.613 --> 00:01:55.750
"Workaholics" fans are familiar with the comedic
stylings of Erik Griffin, who played Montez

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:59.000
Walker on the series, appearing in some 44
episodes.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.460
But one woman in Kentucky, it seems, is decidedly
not a fan.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.810
In a 2016 clip that went viral, Griffin had
a wince-worthy confrontation during a comedy

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.540
set at The Laughing Derby in Louisville.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.450
The woman, sitting in the front row, had apparently
been heckling Griffin throughout his set.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:16.160
Things came to a head when he began addressing
her from the stage, and as comedy club employees

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.200
and patrons urged her to leave, she became
increasingly indignant.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.469
According to TMZ, she eventually resorted
to taking a swing at Griffin, and she was

00:02:23.469 --> 00:02:26.010
tossed out of the club just as police arrived.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.020
For two of the series stars, confusion with
their names has presented some difficulty

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.560
as their famous quasi-doppelgangers have landed
in hot water.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.530
Blake Anderson, for instance, shares a name
with the football coach at Utah State.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.610
This would be a minor annoyance, perhaps,
but it got worse in 2021, when coach Blake

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:46.080
Anderson was recorded telling his players
that it, quote, "has never been more glamorized

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.909
to be a victim" of sexual assault.

00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:51.510
For understandable reasons, the name "Blake
Anderson" received some hate on the internet

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.670
in the aftermath of the scandal.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:57.150
Adam DeVine, meanwhile, takes it in stride
that those who approach him often mistake

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.480
him for Maroon 5 frontman Adam Levine.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.640
While some might see being mistaken for a
sex symbol musician as a blessing, when Levine

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.410
admitted in 2022 that he had, quote, "crossed
the line" with a TikTok influencer named Sumner

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:13.000
Stroh, the association suddenly required some
distancing, with DeVine taking to Instagram

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.510
to make a lighthearted post saying -

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.830
"My wife [Chloe Bridges] and I are doing great
and going strong.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.470
I am not Adam Levine.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.140
He's a different guy and a worse singer.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.631
We are however naming our future baby Sumner."

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.840
"Workaholics" often paralleled the lives of
its creators, and that was true for Anders

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.830
Holm, whose character was depicted throughout
the series as having an unusually over-the-top

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.040
affinity for swimming.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.660
Much of that aquatic enthusiasm was based
on the actor's real-world experiences.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:41.209
In actuality, Holm is a talented swimmer;
he was on an impressive track when he swam

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:43.709
for two years at the University of Wisconsin.

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:47.730
Unfortunately, his sports career did not end
well, as he revealed in 2022 on the swimming

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.739
podcast "Inside with Brett Hawke."

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:53.960
"I made a mistake and got in a bar fight and
was terminated from the team."

00:03:54.092 --> 00:03:55.642
"Oh really you were booted?"

00:03:55.642 --> 00:03:56.764
"Yeah I got booted."

00:03:56.764 --> 00:04:00.770
These days, Holm continues to harness his
talents in aquatics; he competed in the Malibu

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:04.430
Triathlon in 2022, finishing 62nd overall.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:08.159
Holm credits the work ethic he developed from
swimming for giving him the drive to succeed

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:09.200
in his comedy career.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:13.730
Each of the members of "Workaholics," post
series, has managed to build and sustain their

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:15.879
film and television careers in one way or
another.

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:19.440
But one of the trio's most notable cameos
was performed together — when the group

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.590
made an appearance in the 2014 comedy hit
"Neighbors."

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:26.420
In the Nicholas Stoller-directed film, while
recounting the storied history of their fraternity

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:31.020
Delta Psi Beta, Dave Franco and Zac Efron's
characters describe the decades-past adventures

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:32.920
of their frat forefathers.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:37.790
Viewers see "The Lonely Island" trio inventing
the toga party in 1930, and then the "Workaholics"

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.410
group in 1971, inventing beer pong.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:44.990
As a quid pro quo for that latter appearance,
"Neighbors" stars Seth Rogen and Zac Efron

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:49.414
cameoed in a short marketing clip for "Workaholics"
ahead of its 2014 season finale.

00:04:49.919 --> 00:04:55.050
Long-standing "Workaholics" fans were thrilled
when Paramount announced in 2021 that a "Workaholics"

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:56.650
reboot movie was in the works.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:58.360
Alas, it was not to be.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:02.820
Mere weeks before the film was set to launch
from its 2023 starting line, the rug was suddenly,

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.010
unceremoniously pulled out from under the
"Workaholics" boys.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.580
DeVine took to Instagram to share his disappointment,
writing -

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:13.729
"Welp, Paramount+ decided to cancel the Workaholics
movie [...] [Paramount+] told us we don't

00:05:13.729 --> 00:05:15.440
fit their new 'global' strategy.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.139
We are deeply butt hurt about this decision
because we were so excited to bring the weird

00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:20.250
one last time.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:22.740
I'm butt hurt that I don't get to work with
my best friends again."

00:05:23.364 --> 00:05:27.970
Fate put Adam DeVine through a tumultuous
path on his way to helping create "Workaholics."

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:31.690
When DeVine was still in grade school, he
was in a severe automobile accident that nearly

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.060
took his life.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.550
In an interview with "The Off Camera Show,"
he explained that when he was 11, he was hit

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:40.039
by a cement truck while attempting to cross
the street to meet his friend.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:44.889
"Crushed my legs from the knees down and took
all the skin off.

00:05:45.433 --> 00:05:46.551
Like, broke my ribs.

00:05:46.551 --> 00:05:47.440
Punctured lungs."

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.780
The event resulted in a two-week medically
induced coma and 26 surgeries over the next

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:52.810
few years.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:57.410
The tragic incident that nearly took DeVine's
life surely changed things, as DeVine elaborated

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.430
on the "Armchair Expert" podcast, saying -

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:02.220
"I think that's kind of why I got into comedy.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:05.910
After that, I couldn't do anything, anytime
anyone would make fun of me, my dad was like,

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.550
'You can't get into fights so you gotta punch
them back with your words, think of some funny

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.099
things to say back to them.'"

00:06:11.099 --> 00:06:15.000
DeVine spent years writing comebacks and insults
in notebooks which inevitably brought him

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.040
to performing comedy.

00:06:16.814 --> 00:06:20.846
The boys from "Workaholics" spend most of
their days working the phones at TelAmeriCorp.

00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:25.250
Telemarketing is rarely represented on television,
but the boring office job helped emphasize

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.805
the amount of slacking from the "Workaholics."

00:06:27.805 --> 00:06:31.660
Unsurprisingly, the creators of the show captured
the real-world monotony of telemarketing from

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:32.930
their own backgrounds.

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:36.380
While appearing on "What's Trending," Holm
and DeVine each revealed that they had worked

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:39.990
in telemarketing before achieving their big
break and joked about how the portrayal is

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.370
seen by fans.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:45.220
"Like telemarketers will come up to me and
be like 'it's so real, thank you.'"

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:46.460
"This is my life!"

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.680
They did take away a couple of jokes that
made it into the series, such as manipulating

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.940
the elderly into making purchases over the
phone.

00:06:53.489 --> 00:06:57.520
Making a television series was always the
goal for the crew behind Mail Order Comedy.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.770
Unfortunately, they forgot to put someone
in charge of checking their messages in case

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:04.750
such an opportunity arose, as DeVine explained
in an interview with Collider, saying -

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:09.220
"Comedy Central came to us and sent us a message
on YouTube, which we didn't check for about

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:10.220
a month.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:13.039
[...] And then, one day, we were editing a
video and we all showed up to do the edit

00:07:13.039 --> 00:07:16.740
session and Kyle [Newacheck] was like, 'We
need to contact Comedy Central!'"

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.639
Newacheck went on to add that even then, they
weren't in a hurry to respond, saying:

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:24.349
"The weirdest thing was that we weren't excited
enough to figure out how to contact them.

00:07:24.349 --> 00:07:26.000
We waited a week to figure it out."

00:07:26.473 --> 00:07:30.009
Audiences love the three stars that are the
primary focus of "Workaholics."

00:07:30.009 --> 00:07:33.720
However, it was one of their coworkers that
became a resounding fan favorite.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.460
Jillian Bell played the office assistant who
perfectly navigated the work-life balance

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:40.560
between the slacker trio and their boss, Alice
Murphy.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.370
Her placement in the series was completely
intentional for the creators who saw her as

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:47.030
their secret weapon, as Anderson told Vanity
Fair:

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:48.960
"We always knew that she had to be in it.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.159
She was actually part of another group, and
we poached her because we needed a female

00:07:52.159 --> 00:07:53.710
presence in our Internet videos.

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:57.990
Jillian, being one of the funniest people
on the planet, man or woman, stuck out."

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:01.150
Unfortunately, the comedic powerhouse that
is Jillian Bell could not be contained to

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.300
sitcom television for very long.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.220
By the last couple of seasons of "Workaholics,"
fans noticed that Bell was becoming more absent

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.729
from the series, and only appeared in one
episode in the last year.

00:08:10.729 --> 00:08:14.629
As it turns out, Bell's career was moving
too fast as she landed various and high profile

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:15.629
film roles.

00:08:15.707 --> 00:08:19.199
For those who have ever been curious about
where the "Workaholics" crew get all of their

00:08:19.199 --> 00:08:21.539
amazing material, there is good news.

00:08:21.539 --> 00:08:25.560
The boys revealed their trade secret back
in 2014 when they made yet another appearance

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:26.297
on "Conan."

00:08:26.297 --> 00:08:31.917
"I hate to let the cat out of the bag and
tell everyone where we get our secrets but

00:08:31.917 --> 00:08:32.841
... internet porno."

00:08:32.841 --> 00:08:36.469
Hopefully, the trio is making a hilarious
joke, but that is part of what makes them

00:08:36.469 --> 00:08:37.870
special and unique.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.529
The crew behind "Workaholics" never take themselves
too seriously.

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:44.130
In nearly every interview that can be found,
the group is quick to make jokes and play

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.130
off each other's immaturity.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:48.410
In truth, that is where their writer's room
flourishes.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.720
This group of friends spends most of their
time together trying to make each other laugh,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:56.482
and thankfully, that translates to rib-cracking
comedy and audiences cannot get enough.

00:08:56.899 --> 00:09:01.500
There has been a serious gap in television
content since the series ended back in 2017.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.940
Thankfully, the four jokesters that created
the series agree that the world could use

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:06.890
more of their unique brand of comedy.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:11.112
In 2020, all four original members of Mail
Order Comedy decided it was time for a new

00:09:11.112 --> 00:09:12.271
type of project.

00:09:12.271 --> 00:09:15.540
That year they launched their first podcast,
"This is Important."

00:09:15.709 --> 00:09:19.360
The podcast series is not another skit from
the group, but rather a peek at their everyday

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.510
conversations and behind-the-scenes workings.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:25.459
"This is Important" does not cover anything
of real importance with episodes covering

00:09:25.459 --> 00:09:30.490
in-depth conversations about warm toilet seats
or the best appetizers from Outback Steakhouse.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:34.850
Under the iHeart Radio brand, "This is Important"
is already over 100 episodes deep with new

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:36.430
episodes released weekly.

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:40.382
Meanwhile, you can watch the episodes as they
are released on the podcast's YouTube channel.

